drm_sysctl_init: do not assume every DRM device is PCI

drm_add_busid_modesetting() runs for every drm_dev_register() and starts with

	struct pci_dev *pdev = to_pci_dev(dev->dev);
	domain = pci_domain_nr(pdev->bus);
	bus    = pdev->bus->number;

which is only valid when the DRM device's parent really is a pci_dev. For a
platform/SoC DRM driver -- every render-only SoC GPU, and hal/bzkms's
hypervisor-window KMS device -- to_pci_dev() walks backwards from a struct
device that is not embedded in a pci_dev, so ->bus is whatever happens to be
there. On bzdOS/Chimp with a statically allocated parent it is NULL, and
drm_dev_register() panics reading offset 8:

	far = 0x0000000000000008, esr = 0x0000000096000004
	#5 sysctl_add_oid+0x238
	#6 drm_sysctl_init+0x22c
	#7 drm_debugfs_init+0xb4
	#8 drm_minor_register+0xa4
	#9 drm_dev_register+0xdc

hal/lima survives the same code only because its parent sits inside a
heap-allocated platform_device, so the backwards walk lands in readable
adjacent memory and the busid string is silently garbage. That is luck, not
correctness.

Guard the PCI-specific part with dev_is_pci() (linuxkpi already provides it).

The busid STRING deliberately stays PCI-shaped for non-PCI devices, and that is
not laziness -- it was measured. Emitting the honest "platform:<dev->unique>"
breaks Mesa outright: its loader identifies the device from the shape of this
busid (drmGetBusid -> drmParseSubsystemType), has no platform path on FreeBSD,
and answers "MESA-LOADER: failed to retrieve device information" followed by
eglInitialize -> 0x3001. On a Mali-400 with Mesa 26.2 the honest string means no
GL at all. So what this patch fixes is the DEREFERENCE, which is the part that
panics; a fixed 0000:00:00.0 is also strictly better than what non-PCI devices
got before, which was whatever adjacent heap memory happened to contain.

Making the honest string usable is a userspace change -- teaching libdrm's
drmParseSubsystemType and Mesa's loader about platform devices on FreeBSD -- and
belongs in a separate patch against those, not here.

# UPDATE 2026-08-21: the fallback device number now VARIES per DRM device.
# It used to be a fixed "pci:0000:00:00.0" for every one, which is fine with a
# single DRM device and breaks with two: a render-capable driver (lima) and a
# display-only one (bzkms) then advertise the SAME busid, Mesa's loader cannot
# tell them apart, and every GL program fails with "MESA-LOADER: failed to
# retrieve device information" / eglInitialize 0x3001. Observed exactly that
# with both loaded. The sysctl node index -- already this device's unique slot
# under hw.dri -- is used as the PCI device field, which keeps the PCI-shaped
# string Mesa insists on while making it unique.
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_sysctl_freebsd.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_sysctl_freebsd.c
@@ -226,15 +226,62 @@
 {
 	struct sysctl_oid *oid;
 	int domain, bus, slot, func;
-	struct pci_dev *pdev = to_pci_dev(dev->dev);
 
-	domain = pci_domain_nr(pdev->bus);
-	bus    = pdev->bus->number;
-	slot   = PCI_SLOT(pdev->devfn);
-	func   = PCI_FUNC(pdev->devfn);
+	/*
+	 * NOT every DRM device is PCI. A platform/SoC driver's parent is not
+	 * embedded in a struct pci_dev, so to_pci_dev() walks backwards off an
+	 * unrelated struct and ->bus is whatever happens to be there -- NULL on
+	 * a statically allocated parent, which panics here reading offset 8
+	 * during drm_dev_register(). Guard it and fall back to the device's own
+	 * name, which drm has already stored in dev->unique.
+	 */
+	if (dev_is_pci(dev->dev)) {
+		struct pci_dev *pdev = to_pci_dev(dev->dev);
 
-	snprintf(dev->busid_str, sizeof(dev->busid_str),
-	    "pci:%04x:%02x:%02x.%d", domain, bus, slot, func);
+		domain = pci_domain_nr(pdev->bus);
+		bus    = pdev->bus->number;
+		slot   = PCI_SLOT(pdev->devfn);
+		func   = PCI_FUNC(pdev->devfn);
+		snprintf(dev->busid_str, sizeof(dev->busid_str),
+		    "pci:%04x:%02x:%02x.%d", domain, bus, slot, func);
+	} else {
+		/*
+		 * DELIBERATE COMPATIBILITY LIE, and it must stay one until
+		 * userspace can cope. The honest string here is
+		 * "platform:<dev->unique>", and emitting it BREAKS Mesa: its
+		 * loader identifies the device from the shape of this busid
+		 * (drmGetBusid -> drmParseSubsystemType), has no platform path
+		 * on FreeBSD, and answers "MESA-LOADER: failed to retrieve
+		 * device information" followed by eglInitialize -> 0x3001.
+		 * Measured on a Mali-400 with Mesa 26.2: honest busid = no GL
+		 * at all.
+		 *
+		 * So the FORMAT stays PCI-shaped for every device. What this
+		 * patch actually fixes is the DEREFERENCE above: reading
+		 * pdev->bus->number through a struct that is not a pci_dev
+		 * panics (far = 0x8) on any platform DRM driver. A fixed
+		 * 0000:00:00.0 is also strictly better than what non-PCI
+		 * devices got before, which was whatever adjacent heap memory
+		 * happened to contain.
+		 *
+		 * Doing this properly means teaching libdrm's
+		 * drmParseSubsystemType (and Mesa's loader) about platform
+		 * devices on FreeBSD -- a userspace change, not a kernel one.
+		 *
+		 * THE DEVICE NUMBER MUST VARY (2026-08-21). This was a fixed
+		 * "pci:0000:00:00.0" for every device, which is fine with one
+		 * DRM device and breaks with two: a render-capable driver and a
+		 * display-only one then advertise the SAME busid, Mesa's loader
+		 * cannot tell them apart, and every GL program fails with
+		 * "MESA-LOADER: failed to retrieve device information".
+		 * Observed exactly that with lima + bzkms both loaded. Using
+		 * the sysctl node index (which is already this device's unique
+		 * slot under hw.dri) as the PCI device field keeps the shape
+		 * Mesa insists on while making the string unique per device.
+		 */
+		snprintf(dev->busid_str, sizeof(dev->busid_str),
+		    "pci:0000:00:%02x.0", dev->sysctl_node_idx & 0x1f);
+	}
 	oid = SYSCTL_ADD_STRING(ctx, SYSCTL_CHILDREN(top), OID_AUTO, "busid",
 	    CTLFLAG_RD, dev->busid_str, 0, NULL);
 	if (oid == NULL)
